# The layers that are built from parts. One row per `asrt:Fusion`, keyed (filing, layer): the set of
# layers a composition rule can be owed on at all, and what the composer observed that makes the
# parts one layer.
#
# Read from the fusion, not put back together from its parts
#   A fusion names at least one part, so the parts' distinct composed layers are this set too; that
#   is the XSD's `minOccurs`, and algebra/fusions_have_parts.sqlc holds it. The fusion is where
#   `observed` lives, which no part carries.
#
# What is not here was measured directly
#   Set this against layers/remainder.sqlc and the layers missing from it are the ones somebody
#   measured directly rather than assembled. Every fusion calculation in this repository runs over
#   this relation and stops at that boundary.
#
# A reached layer missing from it is at the bottom
#   That is how composition/leaves.sqlc is defined: a reached layer with no row here is where a
#   sum's walk bottoms out. It is an anti-join rather than a `NOT EXISTS`, because "the layers that
#   are fusions" is the relation worth naming and "the layers that are not" follows from it.
#
# A remainder's walk stops elsewhere
#   A remainder's walk bottoms out earlier, and this relation does not name that boundary;
#   composition/unsettled.sqlc does. A row here is a fusion whatever quantity is being added up;
#   whether a walk should stop on it depends on the quantity.
-- asrt:Fusion: the composed layer it names, and asrt:observed.
SELECT f.composition AS filing, f.composed_layer AS layer, f.observed
FROM pm.fusion f
